===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# [[abundance]]; the state of being [[profuse]]; profession. a [[cornucopia]]
#: {{ux|en|His hair, in great '''profusion''', streamed down over his shoulders.}}
#*{{RQ:Aston HJL|Preface|The Italian adage is particularly applicable to translators from the Japanese. Even when they have a competent knowledge of the language they cannot possibly reproduce all the metaphors, allusions, quotations, and illustrations which form the stock of the Japanese author, and which are in great part unintelligible without a '''profusion''' of explanatory notes intolerable to the reader.}}
#* {{RQ:Burroughs Land That Time Forgot||VI|passage=We set the men at work felling trees, selecting for the purpose jarrah, a hard, weather-resisting timber which grew in '''profusion''' near by.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1951|month=November|author=R. K. Kirkland|title=The Wimbledon and West Croydon Line of the Southern Region|journal=Railway Magazine|page=721|text=Although houses and factories appeared in great '''profusion''' in the 1930s, there still remain odd groups of cottages dating from an earlier and more countrified period.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=October|author=Brian Haresnape|title=Focus on B.R. passenger stations|journal=Modern Railways|pages=250–251|text=Elegant brick and stone buildings, with iron and glass canopies and decorative wooden scalloping and fencing&mdash;all evidencing care on the part of the architect to produce a pleasing, well-planned building&mdash;were submerged beneath a '''profusion''' of ill-conceived additions and camouflaged by vulgar paint schemes; and the original conception was lost.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=Jul 18 2022|title=[https://www.csmonitor.com/Commentary/the-monitors-view/2022/0718/Italian-pride-in-a-leader-s-humility Italian pride in a leader's humility]|journal=The Christian Science Monitor
|passage=Elected leaders face a '''profusion''' of mega-pressure points these days – inflation, heat waves, high debt, or the pandemic.}}
# [[lavish]] or [[imprudent]] [[expenditure]]; [[prodigality]] or [[extravagance]], profession.

